<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Antigua Guatemala</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Antigua <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> is a city in the central highlands of Guatemala, founded in 1543 as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> de los Caballeros after the destruction of the previous capital. For over two centuries it was the capital of the Captaincy General of Guatemala, rich in churches and convents. After the earthquakes of 1773 the capital moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala-city">Guatemala City</a>; the ruined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> city is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/antigua-guatemala.jpg" alt="Antigua Guatemala" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Juan Francisco · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Antigua_-_Arco.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Antigua <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> lies in the Panchoy Valley of the central highlands, and three large volcanoes dominate its horizon. The Volcán de Agua, about 3,766 metres high, rises to the south; it took its Spanish name after a lahar from it buried the second site of the capital in 1541. To the west are Acatenango and the Volcán de Fuego, which is almost constantly active at a low level.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first capital was founded on 25 July 1524 at the Kaqchikel Maya city of Iximche and named Ciudad de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> de los Caballeros de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>. After Kaqchikel uprisings it was moved in 1527 to the Valley of Almolonga, where a lahar destroyed it on 11 September 1541. The authorities moved the capital eight kilometres north, and on 10 March 1543 Spanish conquistadors founded the present city, again named Santiago de los Caballeros. For 230 years it was the seat of the Captaincy General of Guatemala, which covered the present Guatemala, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belize">Belize</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-salvador">El Salvador</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honduras">Honduras</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaragua">Nicaragua</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/costa-rica">Costa Rica</a> and the state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiapas">Chiapas</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>. It was laid out in a grid around a central square.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franciscans">Franciscans</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> and the Brothers of San Juan de Dios founded convents, schools and hospitals, and the Royal University of San Carlos Borromeo began classes on 7 January 1681. Earthquakes damaged the city repeatedly, notably in 1717 and 1751. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-marta">Santa Marta</a> earthquakes of 1773 the Crown ordered the capital moved in 1776 to the Valley of the Shrine, the site of the present <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala-city">Guatemala City</a>. The abandoned city was called la Antigua Guatemala. Its population, at a peak of about 65,000 in the 1770s, fell as most residents left.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Antigua is the capital of Sacatepéquez Department. It was declared a National Monument on 30 March 1944 and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1979, for its Spanish Baroque-influenced architecture, layout, churches and ruins. The Arco de Santa Catalina and the Palace of the Captaincy General, which houses the National Museum of Guatemalan Art, survive among the ruins. The city is known for its Holy Week processions over carpets of dyed sawdust and flowers.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Antigua Guatemala” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Republic of Guatemala inherited the core of the Kingdom, or Captaincy General, of Guatemala, the Spanish jurisdiction that governed Central America from Santiago de los Caballeros (today <b>Antigua Guatemala</b>) and, after the earthquakes of 1773, from the new capital of Guatemala City.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-guatemala">Republic of Guatemala · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>An earthquake of an estimated magnitude of 7.4 destroyed much of the architecture of <b>Antigua Guatemala</b>, then the colonial capital of Central America, ruining over 3,000 buildings.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-29">29 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Pedro de Alvarado conquered the highland Maya kingdoms from 1524 and founded <b>Santiago de los Caballeros</b>, first at Iximche.</blockquote><a href="/state/captaincy-general-of-guatemala">Captaincy General of Guatemala</a></li></ul></section>
